# .dockerignore — keep the build context small and free of host state.
#
# The Dockerfile does `COPY . .` in its builder stage, so without this file the
# entire working tree is sent to the daemon on every build. That includes
# ~53 MB of locally built Windows binaries (levee.exe, docgen.exe) which are
# not in .gitignore's *tracked* set but do exist in a developer's checkout and
# would be shipped into the build context — slowing every build and risking a
# stale binary ending up in a layer.

# VCS & CI
.git
.github
.gitattributes
.gitignore

# Build artifacts
/levee
/dist/
*.exe
*.exe~
*.test
*.out
coverage*
coverfunc.txt
docgen.exe
test_*.log

# Local data — runtime state must never be baked into an image
*.db
*.db-wal
*.db-shm
*.log
levee-data/
configs/config.local.yaml

# Secrets
.env
.env.*

# Editor / OS
.idea/
.vscode/
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.swp

# Agent tooling state
.zcode/
.workbuddy/
# protoc scratch dirs (gen-proto.sh cleans these, but just in case)
.protoc-out.*.tmp/

# Docs & tests are not needed to compile the binary.
docs/
tests/
CONTRIBUTING.md
CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md
CHANGELOG.md

# Frontend build inputs are EXCLUDED, not the whole web/ directory — the image
# builds the UI from source. These two are load-bearing, not tidiness:
#   web/node_modules — the Dockerfile runs `npm ci` and then `COPY web/ .`, so
#     without this the host's node_modules is copied OVER the fresh install,
#     shipping a Windows/linux-mismatched dependency tree into a Linux image
#     and silently invalidating the layer cache.
#   web/dist — likewise rebuilt inside the image; the committed copy is
#     internal/web/dist, which the builder stage overwrites after `npm run
#     build` anyway.
web/node_modules/
web/dist/
